#{[#edb/ref 17592186050628 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065053} :activity/id #uuid "6d74a7cb-443e-5352-8f4a-dbb47e0c0820" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065424 :tutorial/content "We know how to calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x^5 \\, \\textrm{d}x.$ But suppose that we want to calculate an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int (2x+1)^5 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe integral above is different from what we've seen previously because now it's a whole function $(2x+1)$ that is being raised to the power of $5.$ So what do we do? \n\nIn this case, the answer is to introduce a new variable. Our goal is to replace all of the $x$'s in the integral, *including* the $\\textrm d x$, with this new variable which we'll call $u.$\n\nFirst, we let $u=2x+1.$ Differentiating both sides with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2,\n$$\n\nand if we treat $\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}$ like a fraction, we can get the $\\textrm d x$ on its own as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d} x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to write our integral in terms of the new variable $u,$ which we do as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+1)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate our integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(2x+1)^{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{12}(2x+1)^{6}+C] & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x+1)^{6}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 6(2x+1)^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+1)^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2x+1)^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064203 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064071 :question/id #uuid "eff323d1-4e70-5490-8e41-9fbf00c387f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-446" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int (3x+1)^3 \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(3x+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 4(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(3x+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064204 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064072 :question/id #uuid "e469c042-9619-55d4-b745-d4ed26251d80" :question/math-academy-id "e-447" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2}{(1-x)^3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2(1-x)^{-3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =  -\\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2(1-x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{u}^{-3}\\cdot (-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {u}^{-3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{-2}}{-2}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =(1-x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-2(1-x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064075 :question/id #uuid "e416cb5c-22df-55e4-a3cb-c6b5dfc650c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-448" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int\\sqrt{1-5x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065427 :tutorial/content "By using the substitution $u=ax+b$, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\int (ax+b)^n\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{1}{a(n+1)}(ax+b)^{n+1} + C, \\qquad n\\neq -1.\n$$\n\nIf you can remember the formula, great! You'll save yourself some time. If you don't like memorizing formulas, you can always solve these integrals using the substitution method described in this lesson." :tutorial/title "The General Formula"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064425}] [#edb/ref 17592186050489 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069899} :activity/id #uuid "afe6da6e-0855-538d-ac54-1e34f990246c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070016 :tutorial/content "We know how to integrate $\\cos{x}.$ But how do we integrate $\\cos{3x}?$ \n\nTo compute\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{3x} \\, \\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe can use substitution. If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068915 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068567 :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068568 :question/id #uuid "daf5cc74-4c7d-500a-9345-03bd6b73a333" :question/math-academy-id "e-465" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 5 \\csc {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\cot {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070017 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068569 :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068570 :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186069001}] [#edb/ref 17592186050101 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097492} :activity/id #uuid "c84ecacf-b4b1-59e2-bb1d-796cf178ed22" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097917 :tutorial/content "We know how to integrate $e^x,$ but how do we integrate a function like $e^{2x}?$\n\nThe answer is to use a substitution. If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096454 :question/id #uuid "9a42787b-24fb-50c8-9f3f-9e95d35c403c" :question/math-academy-id "e-459" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int e^{1-3x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=1-3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{1-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{1-3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096455 :question/id #uuid "cbf09123-3650-5d6a-869a-8edd8f54206b" :question/math-academy-id "e-460" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int 3^{5+4x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5+4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 4 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {3}^{5+4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {3}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{5+4x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097918 :tutorial/content "If you're comfortable with memorizing formulas, then it's worth noting that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C, \\\\ \\int {a}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}a}{a}^{px+q}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf you don't like to memorize formulas, you can always use substitution to solve these types of integrals." :tutorial/title "General Formulas for Integrating Exponential Functions With Linear Exponents"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Linear Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096891}] [#edb/ref 17592186050985 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116045} :activity/id #uuid "b360f8db-39eb-5e92-8961-f79b03985c78" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116473 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sec x$ | $\\sec x\\tan x$ |\n| $\\csc x$ | $-\\csc x\\cot x$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $-\\csc^2 x$ |\n\nAs with the standard trigonometric functions, we can now differentiate any combination of the above functions using the rules of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny= \\sec x + \\csc x.\n$$\n\nWe can take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x+\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could have computed the derivative of the above function by rewriting it as\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand applying the appropriate derivative rules. However, this would have taken significantly more work! You can save a lot of time by memorizing the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116214}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115019 :question/id #uuid "118a33c0-5831-586e-a0a5-e8bd3e1e4c9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3644" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2 \\csc x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115020 :question/id #uuid "23b1cfcf-9f46-5274-b07b-66770a827409" :question/math-academy-id "e-3646" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y} {\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 2x - 3\\cot x.$" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3cot⁡x) \\\\  & =2-3(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2+3{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115021 :question/id #uuid "9b2548d6-87fc-56b1-aa7d-cef328eb4e24" :question/math-academy-id "e-3647" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = x\\sec x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xsec⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sec⁡x) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot (sec⁡xtan⁡x) \\\\  & =sec⁡x(1+xtan⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115022 :question/id #uuid "83b8a6dd-c8c4-5537-ac88-289265752755" :question/math-academy-id "e-3648" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ if $f(x) = \\dfrac{-2\\cot x}{\\sin x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{-2cot⁡x}{sin⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x-(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)}{{[sin⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x+2cot⁡x\\cdot (cos⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2csc⁡x+2cot⁡xcos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{2csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})+2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116474 :tutorial/content "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Justifying the Derivatives of Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116214 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116524 :tutorial/content "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & ={sec}^{2}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x) & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}x) & =-{csc}^{2}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with." :tutorial/title "Mnemonic Tricks"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116046}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115457}]}
